<!--
  BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.documentation.ImplementationNamespace
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/schema/block"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/schema/block https://blockml.org/schemas/core/block.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.core"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.documentation">

  <idea>Domain-wide separation of Block and implementation namespaces via the reserved .impl root.</idea>

  <documentation>
    Each root domain defines exactly two clearly separated roots:

    &lt;domain&gt;.*       → BML / Block namespace (semantic model)
    &lt;domain&gt;.impl.*  → manifestations / technical implementation (reserved)

    Principle: a domain owns one semantic namespace and one reserved implementation namespace.
    Blocks describe what exists. Implementations describe how it is manifested.

    Block namespace — all BML Block FQNs live under the domain root, never under .impl:
    • org.blockml.core.Block
    • org.blockml.type.Integer
    • org.blockml.compiler.parser.Parser
    • org.blockml.compiler.validator.Validator
    • com.machineblocks.bricks.StandardBrick

    Implementation namespace — concrete manifestations and toolchain types live exclusively under
    &lt;domain&gt;.impl.*:
    • org.blockml.impl.parser.XmlParser
    • org.blockml.impl.validation.DefaultValidator
    • org.blockml.impl.serialization.BmlSerializer
    • org.blockml.impl.bom.BlockDefinition
    • com.machineblocks.impl.renderer.ScadBlockProcessor

    The implementation root is domain-wide — not per library. Implementation structure may be
    organized independently of Block structure. Structural mirroring is allowed but not required:
    org.blockml.compiler.parser.Parser may map to org.blockml.impl.compiler.parser.DefaultParser
    or to org.blockml.impl.frontend.CompilerFrontend — both are valid.

    Reservation rule — the &lt;domain&gt;.impl branch is exclusively for manifestations and
    technical implementation. Within BML:
    • no Block may have an FQN under &lt;domain&gt;.impl
    • no BML namespace (xmlns) may point to &lt;domain&gt;.impl
    • no Library may export Blocks from &lt;domain&gt;.impl
    Violation → BML_RESERVED_IMPLEMENTATION_NAMESPACE.

    impl is not a manifestation model — it is simply a reserved technical space. No separate
    manifestation library, mapping layer, or artificial mirroring of Block packages is required.

    Domain examples:
    • org.blockml.* / org.blockml.impl.*
    • com.machineblocks.* / com.machineblocks.impl.*
    • com.martianmicro.* / com.martianmicro.impl.*
  </documentation>

  <references>
    <see file="00_bom_core.md">
      Domain-Scoped Namespace Separation — authoritative rules, examples, reservation rule.
    </see>
    <see file="01_system.md">
      FQN Namespaces — Blocks vs. Implementation — system-level summary.
    </see>
    <see file="02_mbml.md">
      Namespaces — xmlns bindings and Block FQN resolution.
    </see>
    <see file="15_dev_setup.md">
      Package Conventions — npm packages vs. FQN namespaces, .impl source layout.
    </see>
    <see file="06_compiler.md">
      BML_RESERVED_IMPLEMENTATION_NAMESPACE — validation error code.
    </see>
    <see block="doc:LibrarySystem">
      Libraries, packages, and namespace membership — related reading.
    </see>
    <see block="doc:ModelingGuidelines">
      Next reading step — recommended modeling patterns for Block definitions.
    </see>
  </references>

  <name>ImplementationNamespace</name>
  <type>doc:ImplementationNamespace</type>
  <baseType>core:Documentation</baseType>

</block>
